%I A402194 #12 Nov 27 2026 13:46:56
%S A402194 7,11,13,223,227,229,94273,94291,94307,94309,94823,94837,94873,94903,
%T A402194 94907,94933,94949,94961,95063,95087,95111,95131,95153,95191,95203,
%U A402194 95213,95219,95233,95279,95369,95393,95441,95443,95461,95467,95479,95483,95507,95561,97127
%N A402194 Primes p for which pi_{24;17,23}(p) - pi_{24;1,7}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A402194 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,7} and S = {17,23}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-3}, chi_{-24}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A402194 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-3}(q) + chi_{-24}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A402194 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;17,23}(x) > pi_{24;1,7}(x) has logarithmic density 0.98662, making the race between {1,7} and {17,23} the 14th most biased among the 21 races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A402194 Let D(x) = pi_{24;17,23}(x) - pi_{24;1,7}(x). Below x0 = 8*10^12 there are respectively 34876145777, 34876274835, 34876248066, 34876268167, 34876282644, 34876258940, 34876298151, and 34876294229 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 159326. There are 9 sign-changing zones giving a total of 63288 terms below x0:
%C A402194   (1) a(1..3) = 7-13;
%C A402194   (2) a(4..6) = 223-229;
%C A402194   (3) a(7..57) = 94273-98047 (there are 307 primes a(7) <= p <= a(57) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -9 at p = 96097);
%C A402194   (4) a(58..95) = 3438007-3442651 (there are 70 primes a(58) <= p <= a(95) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -3 at p = 3439633);
%C A402194   (5) a(96..12757) = 1131551359-1271435093 (there are 2533015 primes a(96) <= p <= a(12757) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -429 at p = 1147562719);
%C A402194   (6) a(12758..15753) = 1342040671-1375458463 (there are 669451 primes a(12758) <= p <= a(15753) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -258 at p = 1372999087);
%C A402194   (7) a(15754..44706) = 41241087703-41865761669 (there are 6508387 primes a(15754) <= p <= a(44706) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -623 at p = 41564474137);
%C A402194   (8) a(44707..47639) = 50995783159-51019961051 (there are 185387 primes a(44707) <= p <= a(47639) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -172 at p = 50996521921);
%C A402194   (9) a(47640..63288) = 774789999289-775082872931 (there are 3315739 primes a(47640) <= p <= a(63288) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -730 at p = 774896266657).
%C A402194 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 13212362.
%H A402194 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402194/b402194.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..63288</a> (all terms below 8*10^12)
%H A402194 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402194 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193_1.txt">Sign-changing zones of five prime number races modulo 24</a>.
%H A402194 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402194 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A402194 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A402194 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A402194 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A402194 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==7, k--); if(p%24==17||p%24==23, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A402194 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A402194 nonn,new
%O A402194 1,1
%A A402194 _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_, Nov 15 2026
